# AutomaticWatering

3-zone automatic plant watering system running on an **Arduino UNO R4 WiFi**. Each zone monitors soil moisture and temperature, drives a relay-controlled pump when the soil gets too dry, and stops when it is wet enough. A 240×240 TFT display shows live readings and connectivity status. Arduino IoT Cloud provides remote monitoring and manual pump overrides from anywhere.

## Hardware

| Component | Part | Connection |
|---|---|---|
| Microcontroller | Arduino UNO R4 WiFi | — |
| Display | 240×240 ST7789 TFT | SPI (CS=10, DC=9, RST=8, MOSI=12, SCLK=13) |
| Temperature sensors | NTC thermistors (10 kΩ, β=3950) | A3, A4, A5 |
| Moisture sensors | Resistive humidity sensors | A0, A1, A2 |
| Pumps | Relay modules | D2, D3, D4 |

## Build & Deploy

```bash
pio run                                          # compile only
pio run -t upload                                # compile and flash
pio device monitor -b 9600                       # open serial monitor
pio run -t upload && pio device monitor -b 9600  # flash and monitor
```

## Configuration

Two files are excluded from the repo and must be created locally. Template files are provided for both.

**`src/passwords.h`** — WiFi credentials. Copy the template and fill in your details:

```bash
cp src/passwords.h.example src/passwords.h
```

**`lib/network/thingProperties.h`** — generated by the Arduino IoT Cloud dashboard. Open your Thing on [app.arduino.cc](https://app.arduino.cc), download the full sketch, and copy `thingProperties.h` into `lib/network/`. A template showing the expected structure is available at [`lib/network/thingProperties.h.example`](lib/network/thingProperties.h.example).

All pin assignments, sensor calibration values, pump thresholds, and timing values live in [`include/constants.h`](include/constants.h). The two timing constants worth noting:

| Constant | Default | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `SETUP_DELAY_MS` | 3000 ms | Pause at the end of `setup()` to let the cloud connection stabilise before the main loop starts |
| `LOOP_DELAY_MS` | 5000 ms | Delay at the end of each `loop()` iteration — controls how often sensors are read and the display refreshed |

## How It Works

1. On boot, `setup()` initialises the pumps, display, RTC, and Arduino IoT Cloud connection.
2. `loop()` polls the cloud, reads all six sensors, and refreshes the display every 5 seconds.
3. Once WiFi is available, time is synced from `pool.ntp.org` via NTP and stored in the onboard hardware RTC. The RTC keeps ticking accurately even if the network drops later. Re-sync happens every 24 hours.
4. Pump automation (currently in development) turns a pump ON when humidity falls below 50% and OFF when it reaches 85%. Manual overrides are always available via the IoT Cloud dashboard.

## Project Structure

```
AutomaticWatering/
├── include/
│   └── constants.h                    Pin mappings, thresholds, calibration values
├── src/
│   ├── main.cpp                       setup()/loop(), pump logic, cloud polling
│   ├── passwords.h                    WiFi credentials (gitignored — use template)
│   └── passwords.h.example            Template for passwords.h
├── lib/
│   ├── network/
│   │   ├── thingProperties.h          IoT Cloud setup (gitignored — use template)
│   │   ├── thingProperties.h.example  Template for thingProperties.h
│   │   ├── wifi.cpp / wifi.h          Standalone WiFi helper (unused)
│   ├── pumps/                         Relay control
│   ├── screen/                        TFT display
│   ├── sensors/                       Temperature & humidity readings
│   └── time/                          NTP sync + RTC
├── doc/                               Module documentation
│   ├── sensors.md
│   ├── pumps.md
│   ├── screen.md
│   ├── time_manager.md
│   └── network.md
└── platformio.ini                     Board config, build flags, library dependencies
```

## Modules

### `sensors`
Reads the NTC thermistors (temperature) and resistive humidity sensors for all three zones. Uses the Beta equation for temperature and `map()` for humidity. → [doc/sensors.md](doc/sensors.md)

### `pumps`
Controls the three relay-driven water pumps on pins D2–D4. Threshold-based automatic control with hysteresis, plus manual override for IoT Cloud callbacks. → [doc/pumps.md](doc/pumps.md)

### `screen`
Drives the 240×240 ST7789 TFT display over SPI using `TFT_eSPI`. Renders per-zone flower faces whose expression and colour reflect moisture and temperature, alongside WiFi/BT status indicators. → [doc/screen.md](doc/screen.md)

### `time`
Syncs the current time from `pool.ntp.org` via NTP once WiFi is available, then maintains it using the Arduino UNO R4's onboard hardware RTC. Applies EU DST rules automatically (CEST/CET). → [doc/time_manager.md](doc/time_manager.md)

### `network`
A standalone `connectWifi()` helper. Currently unused — WiFi is managed by `ArduinoIoTCloud` via `WiFiConnectionHandler`. Kept for reference. → [doc/network.md](doc/network.md)

## Dependencies

Declared in `platformio.ini` and installed automatically by PlatformIO:

| Library | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `bodmer/TFT_eSPI` | ST7789 TFT display driver |
| `arduino-libraries/ArduinoIoTCloud` | Cloud connectivity and remote variables |
| `arduino-libraries/NTPClient` | NTP time sync |
| `arduino-libraries/ArduinoHttpClient` | HTTP support for IoT Cloud |
| `knolleary/PubSubClient` | MQTT support for IoT Cloud |

Built-in UNO R4 libraries (`RTC.h`, `WiFiUdp.h`) require no extra installation.
